## Description

Resolves #1549.

Also, fixes a potential segfault where events would get sent to a window after it was destroyed, and removes a spurious error message when destroying a Window that is embedded in an NSView.

SFML's handling of Cocoa autorelease pools does not follow the guidelines in [Apple's developer documentation](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/foundation/nsautoreleasepool).

In particular, SFML creates long-lived autorelease pools (one per thread) that are not guaranteed to be properly nested with other autorelease pools (for example, those created by other GUI frameworks, such as wxWidgets and Qt).

This can cause a crash when the autorelease pools are drained (either SFML's or the other framework's).

The following code is well-formed, yet crashes when closing SFML's window.
```objective-c
#include <SFML/Graphics.hpp>
#import <Cocoa/Cocoa.h>
int main()
{
    NSAutoreleasePool *pool = [[NSAutoreleasePool alloc] init];
    sf::RenderWindow window(sf::VideoMode(1280, 720), "Title");
    [pool drain];
    window.close();
}
```
Similarly, the following example crashes when draining the "external" autorelease pool:
```objective-c
#include <SFML/Graphics.hpp>
#import <Cocoa/Cocoa.h>
int main()
{
    sf::RenderWindow window(sf::VideoMode(1280, 720), "Title");
    NSAutoreleasePool *pool = [[NSAutoreleasePool alloc] init];
    window.close();
    [pool drain];
    return 0;
}
```
The current PR fixes both of these crashes.

---

The approach taken in this PR is to avoid ever creating long-lived autorelease pools. This is a little bit complicated, because SFML does not control the application event loop (unlike some GUI frameworks, users write their own event loops in application code).

Typically, an application would create a new autorelease pool at the beginning of the event loop, and drain it at the end of the loop. This is not possible in SFML without requiring changes in application code. However, autorelease pools still do need to be created whenever Cocoa APIs are called, otherwise memory will leak.

In this PR, a new autorelease pool is created (and then drained) whenever the user calls a function that calls into Cocoa APIs. Basically, any user-facing function that has a Cocoa backend implementation needs to create these autorelease pools (Clipboard, Cursor, Input (keyboard/mouse), Joystick, Context, and Window). This is made more convenient with a C++ wrapper object `AutoreleasePool` that creates a new pool in the constructor, and drains the pool in the destructor.

Unfortunately, this does add some noise to the OSX implementation, but I don't see how it's avoidable given that SFML does not control the event loop.

